last night the final german troops  stationed in afghanistan left the  country  later this week the withdrawal of u.s  troops is expected to be completed  ending a mission that began almost two  decades ago a mission that became  america's longest war  it was the 9 11 terror attacks in the  u.s in 2001 that prompted the u.s led  mission in afghanistan  the target the taliban which controlled  afghanistan at the time  and had given the 911 terrorists a place  to plot  their attacks two trillion dollars later  and countless casualties  among afghan civilians and alliance  forces  can anyone claim mission accomplished  as the withdrawal ends the taliban is  reclaiming  territory quickly the top u.s general  predicted a civil war could erupt within  months  and that could spell doom for the  fragile progress  that was achieved under the protective  umbrella of an alliance  that is now going home  for peter schwedick afghanistan is a  home away from home  for more than 30 years he and his wife  anna marie have been aid workers there  teaching children how to read and write  and do maths both are disappointed with  the international community's  achievements  civilian reconstruction should have  begun at the administrative level  they should have put an administration  in place and kept a close eye on what  they did with all the funding  none of that was checked they just kept  throwing money at it and when things  didn't work out they just threw more  club  say children still don't learn enough at  school  partly because international partners  made mistakes  on an organizational level too  see  despite debate about whether the  intervention of the international  community was worthwhile  there have been marked improvements  especially in education  unesco say the number of kids enrolling  in schools has risen clearly  but the education gap between rural and  urban areas remains  there has been considerable progress at  primary school level  it's estimated that half of all afghans  can now read and write  that was different 20 years ago  the same goes for educating girls in the  1990s under the taliban they got no  education  at all women were completely oppressed  under the taliban their situation  remains precarious  but they have gained some rights they  can vote  join political movements and go into  business  but state-level corruption remains  endemic transparency international ranks  afghanistan  as one of the world's most corrupt  countries one reason why aid money  doesn't always reach its intended  destination  we have to acknowledge that the  international community was not capable  of laying down rules on how the money  was to be spent  they never got around to introducing  good governance or tackling corruption  the security situation remains dramatic  now the taliban is pushing to get back  into power  it already has half the country under  its control  that's why anna marie and peter shritik  expect the situation in afghanistan to  stay  just as it's always been extremely  unstable  i don't see any long-term peace  unless the government reconciles with  the taliban  and tries to get the country sorted out  itself  still the couple plan to go back again  soon  they say the people need their help now  more than ever  my first guest tonight is dr eleanor  cyno the country director for  afghanistan at the german think tank the  conrad  adenauer foundation she joins me from  here in berlin it's good to have you  on the day i want to start by getting  your opinion  on this we've been hearing all day from  the german military that this  was a mission accomplished in  afghanistan do you agree  well i think we don't have accomplished  all of our goals and expectations and  we have stayed below our expectations in  many points  and we just heard about we don't have  security for the afghans in the country  we don't have basic infrastructure in  kabul we have only a few hours of water  and electricity a day  and corruption fighting has not been  successful  so in many points we have not  accomplished i understand that you are  planning to return to kabul  in july does that trip now take on more  risks and dangers for you personally  now that u.s and nato forces have  withdrawn yes of course it  it takes more risk and also it's  completely unpredictable  about the next weeks and months that  what we will see  and my security setup of course is much  more safer than  many afghans have in kabul or in  afghanistan  but it's unpredictable for all of us and  we don't know  under what risks and under what  conditions i will return to kabul  what do you say when you're back in  kabul  if afghans come up to you and say  germany the u.s have abandoned us what's  your response  well i say to them that i have always  said  to my afghan partners and networks i  have always said  you don't have to wait you cannot wait  for an external savior  and an afghan solution a peaceful  solution can only come from inside from  the afghans themselves  and i can only call on the international  community also to place  confidence in the afghans that they can  reach a peaceful settlement but of  course there is no guarantee of success  yeah and what's your feeling from the  the afghans that you talk with  in the country i mean is there any  possibility that a peaceful  resolution can be found with the taliban  well currently the mood is really  shifting and people become much more  pessimistic  and and i can really feel the tension  growing day by day and also the  anxieties  increasing day by day and the problem  it's not only the taliban  there's many armed groups in the country  and many conflict parties  to the conflict and this is the  difficult task  of getting a negotiated settlement where  all of the interests the different  interests  are kind of represented and also  balanced  and so i think the taliban yes they are  now the dominating force  but there are many armed groups and now  we see in re-arming of militias in the  country what i  find quite worrying and that has to  really spell  doom for the future of women's rights in  the country  or does it yes it's of course it's  women's rights it's human rights it's  also  the freedom of media the freedom of  press the freedom of expression  so um i see particularly when we when we  talk about women rights and education  i see more the danger to an islamization  of education of the public life of  public media  and this is the main threat that i see  if we have a government in the future  with the taliban  eleanor zainer from the conrad adenauer  foundation dr santa we appreciate your  time tonight we wish you all the best  on your upcoming trip to kabul stay safe  thank you
 

